## The idea

Fisher–Yates: from the last position down, swap it with a random position **at or before it**. Each ordering comes out with probability exactly 1/n!. Keep a private copy of the original for Reset.

## Step by step

1. Constructor: copy the input twice (original and current).
2. Shuffle: for i from n−1 down to 1, j = random 0..i, swap current[i] and current[j]; return a copy.
3. Reset: copy the original back.

## Worked example

**Input:** [1, 2, 3]

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| i = 2 | j = random 0..2, say 0 → [3,2,1] |
| i = 1 | j = random 0..1, say 1 → unchanged |

**Result:** [3, 2, 1] — one of 6 equally likely results

## Cost

O(n) per shuffle; O(n) memory.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// LeetCode 384 — shuffle an array so every order is equally likely; Reset gives back the original.
public sealed class Shuffler {
    private readonly int[] _original;
    private readonly int[] _current;
    private readonly Random _rnd;

    public Shuffler(int[] nums) : this(nums, new Random()) { }
    public Shuffler(int[] nums, Random rnd) {
        _original = (int[])nums.Clone();           // copies: the caller can change their array safely
        _current = (int[])nums.Clone();
        _rnd = rnd;
    }

    public int[] Reset() {
        Array.Copy(_original, _current, _original.Length);
        return (int[])_current.Clone();
    }

    public int[] Shuffle() {
        // Fisher-Yates: for each position from the end, swap in a random element from the part not yet fixed.
        // Each of the n! orders comes out with probability exactly 1/n!.
        for (int i = _current.Length - 1; i > 0; i--) {
            int j = _rnd.Next(i + 1);              // 0..i inclusive — not 0..n-1, which is biased
            int t = _current[i]; _current[i] = _current[j]; _current[j] = t;
        }
        return (int[])_current.Clone();
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Why is `rnd.Next(n)` biased?**

It produces nⁿ equally likely swap sequences; nⁿ is not a multiple of n! (for n = 3: 27 vs 6), so some orders get more sequences.

**Q: Complexity?**

O(n) per shuffle, O(n) memory.

**Q: Many threads shuffle.**

Each call must work on its own copy; System.Random is not thread-safe — use Random.Shared.

**Q: Shuffle a list too big for memory.**

Assign random keys and external-sort, or shuffle in chunks then randomly interleave.

**Q: Related?**

